            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Mendeleev's Predictions</strong><br>\nMendeleev not only organized the elements but also predicted the discovery of new elements. He forecast the existence and properties of elements like gallium and germanium. When these elements were discovered, their alignment with Mendeleev's predictions substantially proved the validity of his Periodic Table.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Later Works and Achievements</strong><br>\nMendeleev's contributions went beyond the Periodic Table. He investigated the properties of solutions, wrote a seminal textbook on chemistry, and studied petroleum. His work laid the groundwork for modern chemical industry and production. Mendeleev received numerous awards for his work but never the Nobel Prize, which some historians consider an oversight.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Life and Characteristics</strong><br>\nMendeleev was known for his relentless work ethic and broad scientific interests. He was married twice and had a total of six children. His personal life was marked by controversy, including his divorce from his first wife and subsequent marriage to a woman 18 years his junior.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
